WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.040
There are all kinds of situations where

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.120
we would love to have more control

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.760
over what the brain can do,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.840
so that you have these
choices of what exactly you're taking in,

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.000
what you're learning, and where
you're going with that.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.640
And there are also situations where we
simply wish that we could experience more.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.200
Say, for example,

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:52.120
that you would like to be able to perceive
things that we currently can't perceive,

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.200
you want to be able to see

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:58.880
the ultraviolet, or you want to be able
to hear ultrasound or what if you wanted

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.440
to be able to remember more things
than you normally can remember?

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.560
The most exciting part of the future to me

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.160
is the potential to achieve
humanistic intelligence.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.320
This idea that the late Marvin Minsky

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.560
talked about,
which is being able to create

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.600
an environment where we have a symbiotic
relationship with artificial intelligence

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:31.080
and we will see a blurring of our
physical, digital and biological worlds,

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.120
and not so much being separate parts,

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.920
but as being seamless parts
of one integrated human organism.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:48.000
It probably will be possible to read out
what's inside a person's brain,

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.880
to understand what's there by translating
it into something you can understand,

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.040
and maybe even putting it back,
giving it to someone, teaching.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.640
When we make a decision now,

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.560
we are under the impression that we are
the ones who are making the decision,

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.840
and we make it because we want to make it.
But if our brains are connected

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:12.120
to the net, to the computer cloud
and their algorithms that they are using

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.720
are decision making,
well, who is deciding?

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.800
We are curious to understand who we are,
and why we think the way we think,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.920
and what is the reason of our thoughts,

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.240
our memories, our imagination,
our feelings...

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.360
Understanding this incredibly complex

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.560
organ, that gives rise
to everything that makes us human.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.320
Everything we see, feel,
hear... our sense of identity

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.040
and so given the vast complexity

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:43.560
of the brain, in a hundred billion neurons, you know,

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.960
hundreds of trillions of connections,

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.200
operating in a dynamic network
that's constantly changing...

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.920
It's just a really, very,

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.080
very complicated system
for us to study with science.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.440
So, it's a, it's a big challenge,
but one that I really enjoy undertaking.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:09.560
In the human brain, you have about 3
million km of fibers connecting it,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.720
so it's enough to go around the Moon
a couple of times, just in one brain.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:21.880
The place in the universe that is
likely the most complex and mysterious,

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.480
the place where emotions,
thoughts and memories arise,

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.880
that place in the universe that tries
tirelessly to understand itself,

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:33.880
an unsolved enigma.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.960
We don't know and we cannot build today

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:44.440
anything that is even as closely connected
to as what we see in the human brain.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.680
There's one hypothesis that suggests
that what brains are doing, what our

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.760
brain is doing is generating
a virtual reality,

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.160
a model of the world that is
running in our heads all the time,

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.160
this has to do with how ideas that have been

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.200
floating around in philosophy for a few
centuries, in particular

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:21.000
with the suggestion by Emmanuel Kant,
the German philosopher,

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:28.080
that the reason why the world on our
minds, our views of the world agree,

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:32.480
is not because our minds
reflect the world, no, no,

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:36.760
it's the opposite, is that the world
is created by our minds,

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:41.000
in other words, that what we perceive
is not out there,

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.560
it's in here.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.320
It weighs less than 3 pounds.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.840
It consumes scarcely 20 or 30 watts
of power, and nevertheless,

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:58.680
it's able to make calculations
more complex than a supercomputer.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:05.360
There are 100 to 1000 trillion synapses,
about the size of a bacteria,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.320
they basically are chemical machines.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.400
They receive an electric pulse and then

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.520
they release chemicals.
They hit receptors,

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.240
a very complex machinery is triggered.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:20.480
This results in a change
in the behavior of the neurons,

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:24.760
it causes genes to express,
new proteins to come in,

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:31.440
the cascade of biochemical and biophysical
processes is enormously complex.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:45.880
If you were trying to describe a
thousand networks of cells working,

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:52.520
I would say that imagine that you are
having phone calls with 10,000 people,

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:58.360
at one at the same time, and the same what
you are doing, everybody is doing,

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.720
who is living on our planet, on the Earth,
about 7 billion people.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.440
And when you then try to describe all this

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.000
communication that is going on,
of all the 7 billion people.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.080
And then you are meant to that this is only one
10th, perhaps, of the capacity,

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:22.440
and everything is only within your skull,
is restricted to one and a half kilogram,

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:24.600
and this is complexity, in my view.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.600
In spite of 100 years of neuroscience,

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.720
we still don't understand
how the brain works,

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:53.240
we still don't have in a general theory
of the function of the brain.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:59.200
The first reason why
I would argue is critical

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:01.120
to understand how the brain works has

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:05.240
to do with our own
definition as human beings.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.880
We are a mental species,

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:11.280
we are defined by our minds,
not by our bodies,

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.160
so we are a very special type of animal,

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:18.920
we don't understand the organ
that generates our minds,

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:26.360
so when we understand how the brain works,
we will be able to understand how our mind

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.640
is from the inside, we'll understand
ourselves for the first time.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:43.280
The second reason has to do
with the clinic, with patients.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.480
Humankind should understand how the brain

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.640
works because we need, it's urgent that we help
these patients that have mental

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:54.000
and neurological diseases and we have to
understand the system in order to fix it.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:09.720
We estimate that possibly one person out
of two to one person out of three in Europe

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:15.400
have some kind of disorder
that is related to the brain.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.760
Altogether, it's maybe up to more than 200

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:23.400
million person in Europe
who have one of these disorders.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:28.280
And it's also associated
with a huge cost for the society.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:34.640
The cost of all disease affecting
the brain more, than the cost of

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:39.600
all cancer, plus all cardiovascular
disease, plus diabetes.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.200
And then the third reason has to do
with technology and the economy.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:48.480
And it is very likely that brains,

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:51.160
not just of humans,
but brains of all animals,

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:56.240
have discovered through hundreds
of millions of years of evolution

00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:01.400
ways to compute that could be much more
efficient and powerful than what we're

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.560
doing currently with our
digital computers.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:05.840
So, if this is the case, if we understood,

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:09.800
if we were able to break this neural code
and understand

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.240
how the brain computes and what are
the algorithms that are present in our

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:18.520
nervous systems,
we will likely revolutionize computer

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:23.120
science, computer technology, and I
would say the economy at the same time.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.480
The Holy Grail for this will be to create

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:36.360
a much more symbiotic relationship
with artificial intelligence.

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:37.920
As we can see today,

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:41.280
artificial intelligence is moving forward
in leaps and bounds,

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:47.800
offering us a myriad of new ways of being
more productive and more efficient in how

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:52.320
we conduct our businesses and how
we manage our daily lives.

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:55.080
With the introduction of more and more

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:59.680
devices that actually understand the human
and help humans optimize their own

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:04.360
performance, we will have a way to create
a much more a different type

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:07.600
of intelligence, what I would
call humanistic intelligence.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:09.960
The distinction between the biological

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:13.800
world, the physical world,
and the digital world will just blur,

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:18.480
and we will integrate seamlessly
across all of these dimensions.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:21.280
A deep understanding of the functioning

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:26.520
of the human brain could bring answers
to great philosophical questions,

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:32.640
such as what consciousness is and how
it is built, or if there is free will.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:40.320
Concepts such as the inner self, guilt
or even reality could change forever.

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:45.120
We are opening the door to a world where
we will communicate directly

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:50.000
with a computer through thought,
a world where we will be able to share our

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:53.080
thoughts and our emotions
through the Internet.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:56.000
We can envision a future in which we are

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:01.040
able to read the thoughts of others
and even to manipulate them.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:07.920
We're building technology to read

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:13.200
the activity of all the neurons in these
brains and to write activity to control

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.120
and manipulate the activity
of these neurons in the brain.

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:21.520
And these technologies are necessary
to be able to cure human patients.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:27.480
But the same technologies,
technology is neutral.

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:29.680
It could be used for good or for bad.

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:32.440
The same technologies to read and write

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:38.440
activity into brains can be used
to decipher what people are thinking or

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:43.200
to influence the activity
of the minds of people.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.200
So you say, well, this is science fiction?

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:46.960
Well, not really.

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:57.040
In the middle of the 90s,

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:01.560
the last decade of the 20th century,
and the second millennium,

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:05.480
a whole revolution in the world
of neuroscience occurred:

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:09.880
the neuroimaging techniques appeared.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:12.520
So neuroimaging techniques are important

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:17.720
for this revolution in how to learn
and explore the brain,

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:23.080
because it's a nice, invasive
way to get inside the brain.

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:25.160
One of the imaging techniques that had

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:30.560
the greatest impact was
magnetic resonance imaging.

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.880
For the first time ever,
we could see the inner functioning

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:41.320
of the brain while it experiences
sensations and emotions.

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:44.920
In fact, functional magnetic resonance imaging,

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:49.560
able to detect the presence
of oxygen in the blood and the brain

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:53.960
has allowed us to discover something
unimaginable years ago.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.760
How different areas in the brain interact

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:04.160
while the patient talks, sees an image,
listens to a sound, or simply thinks.

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:08.960
But if you ask him to focus on one task,

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:14.160
like listening to a sound,
only the region from this specific task,

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:18.160
the auditory cortex, for example,
will have more conception of oxygen.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.840
And this region will
pop up in the contrast,

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:26.920
if you analyze the data, even listening
to sound against non listening to sound.

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:32.560
The reason why we don't understand how
the brain works is not because of its

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:38.480
complexity, but because we've been lacking
methods to study at the right level.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:42.240
Now, if you think about it,
trying to understand a system as complex

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:47.440
as the brain that has 100 billion
neurons by recording from one neuron.

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:49.000
It's a little bit like trying to watch a movie

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:52.600
in a TV screen that has a million pixels

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.600
by looking at a single pixel.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:59.240
So no matter for how long you study
a single pixel or you study a single neuron,

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:05.320
you will be missing the figures in
the screen and the shapes of the images.

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:08.360
Because they are an emergent property

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:12.680
of the correlations in space and time
between the individual pixels.

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:23.080
We think that neuroscience should embark

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:28.200
on this period of new technology
to develop the method so

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:32.440
that neuroscientists and clinicians will
be able to see for the first time

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:35.960
in the future the activity
of complete neural circuits.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:40.120
And be able to decipher the functional
meaning of this pattern.

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:56.800
How can one measure the activity of each

00:15:56.840 --> 00:16:01.000
and every one of the neurons
in the brain at the same time?

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:04.760
The methods that we and many other
people are using are optical methods.

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:10.160
We use light because light can penetrate
the tissue in a noninvasive fashion.

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:14.280
In particular, the method that I
pioneered is called Calcium imaging.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:18.600
We used it for the first time to measure
the activity of neural circuits.

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:24.520
Every time a neuron fires, every time it's
activated, calcium comes into the neuron.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:27.320
So that means if you have these little
calcium indicators,

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:32.640
these fluorescent molecules in all
the neurons, you can directly see who is

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:36.320
firing, who is being activated
in the entire group at once.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.560
Because you see this as changes

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:44.160
in fluorescent that are happening
in different areas at different times.

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:48.800
Thanks to neuroimaging techniques,
we have learned more about the brain

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:54.440
in the last 15 years than
in the whole history of humankind.

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:57.360
The advances already made allow us

00:16:57.400 --> 00:17:02.400
to think that in the near future, we will
be able to solve neurological damage.

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:07.280
Diseases like Parkinson's or Alzheimer's,
depression or schizophrenia.

00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:08.920
Yeah, I think that the revolution we're

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:14.720
experiencing now is first the birth
and the success of human neuroscience.

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:18.560
We now have fields like cognitive
neuroscience where we use functional brain

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:23.720
imaging and noninvasive brain stimulation
to study the functioning human brain.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:26.440
And that was, I think, the first step.

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:36.560
From my perspective,

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:42.520
I've been doing neuroscience research for
30 years and this is the revolution now.

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:44.880
But now is the time that neuroscience is

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:47.120
starting to reach into
people's lives directly.

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:50.400
And to me, that's what we
need to have a revolution.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:55.040
We need to advance from the lab
into the real world.

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:10.440
This is a software application to be
able to control the puzzle box orbit.

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:15.680
I'll first connect it to the emotive
NCIG headset,

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:17.600
and that's now connected.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:20.760
What we have to do is train two patterns.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:23.520
The first is a resting state so that we

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:28.000
understand when you want
to control the orbit versus not.

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:30.680
And then the second pattern is to control

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:34.120
what you want to apply
for flying the orbit.

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:01.960
This leap from the lab to the real world

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:06.200
can already be seen in the form
of the perfecting of what belonged

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:09.640
to the realm of science
fiction just a few years ago:

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:15.200
brain-computer association,
the so called Brain-Machine Interfaces,

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:20.920
have solidified this revolution
in the world of the neuroscience.

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:24.560
Well, BCI really just means
brain computer interface.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.440
So anything that makes a connection

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:33.800
between processing going on inside your
brain and a machine, some other device.

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:39.920
Brain computer interfaces can be grouped
as a group of technologies that can help

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:45.920
both from a hardware and software
perspective, able to both decode, process,

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:50.640
and activate the interactions
between humans and machines.

00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:53.320
Decoding the brain.
Of course, typical decision would be

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:58.320
a platform that can help us interact
from the brain to the world.

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:16.440
The Brain-Machine interfaces are bringing

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:21.520
great advances in various
therapeutical applications.

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:24.320
Up to now, one of the main practical applications is

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:28.840
the treatment of different pathologies
associated with paralysis.

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:32.760
At the Federal Polytechnic School
of Lausanne, Switzerland, Dr.

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:35.440
José Millán has been working for years

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:39.240
on an ambitious project based
on the development of a brain machine interface

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:43.800
that allows paralytics
to control a robotic exoskeleton

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:49.400
with their minds, thanks to which they
can walk again or move a hand.

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:51.760
The patients wear an electrode helmet

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:56.480
that detects their cerebral
electrical activity.

00:20:57.480 --> 00:21:01.240
What we ask people to do at the very
beginning because they need to go through

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:06.120
a training in order to acquire
the capability to modulate voluntarily

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:12.320
their brain signals, is to imagine the
movement that they want to accomplish.

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:17.480
And this requires some time,
because it is not easy to do that.

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:20.480
Our algorithms are capable to discover

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:24.040
which are the areas,
the specific areas that they are

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:29.520
activating and disactivating
at which frequencies this is happening.

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:34.520
And this is the input to our machine
learning algorithms that will build

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:39.200
the model of the decoder that will
differentiate,

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:45.800
opening a closing of the hand, right step,
left step, and so on and so forth.

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:47.560
So, during this training,

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:52.520
people at the very beginning need to
imagine the movement of their own body.

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:54.520
And in many cases, people,

00:21:54.560 --> 00:21:59.640
after some training,
and because the fact that as they imagine

00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:04.280
the movement of their body,
this is executed via the exoskeleton,

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:07.880
they don't need to think any
longer about their own body.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:12.440
They think in terms of the exoskeleton,
because the exoskeleton has been integrated

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:18.200
somehow in people's
body schema that we have in our brain.

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:24.680
The possibilities that this technique

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:29.040
offers to these patients have allowed
to a quadriplegic patient to be able

00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:34.320
to drive a Formula 1
car just with his mind.

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:41.320
In 2017, Rodrigo Hubner Mendes became the
first person to reach such a milestone.

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:44.160
This opportunity to work
with Rodrigo was incredible.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:51.480
Globo TV in Brazil created a vehicle,
a Formula 1 vehicle that was completely

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:54.640
controlled just using
the power of thought.

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:57.920
So what they used was our 14 channel epoch

00:22:57.960 --> 00:23:03.400
plus headset, measuring electrical
fluctuations from Rodrigo's brain.

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:06.480
He spent a few months mapping and training

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:11.640
the algorithms to understand how he
associated specific thoughts,

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:15.920
whether it be drive forward,
right, turn right, left, or stop.

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:19.040
And then he was able to use those commands

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:23.680
to drive this car completely,
just using his mind.

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:29.120
So no steering wheel, no pedals,
just him inside the vehicle.

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:38.160
This was a very fortuitous moment when

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:41.880
Lewis Hamilton,
who is obviously the world's reigning F1

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:46.600
champion, was in the same
place as Rodrigo in the UAE.

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:51.200
Rodrigo posted a very exciting challenge

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:54.240
for Lewis, which is,
would you like to race me?

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:57.280
Surprisingly,
Lewis accepted the challenge,

00:23:57.320 --> 00:24:01.640
so I'm not sure when we're going to start
seeing the two of them start practicing.

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:06.480
But I know that Rodrigo is very excited
about the ability and the opportunity

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:11.120
to race with the reigning world
champion, just using his mind.

