what they're imagining.

00:41:49.080 --> 00:41:53.800
And you can try to give those imaginings
or those sounds to someone else.

00:42:01.240 --> 00:42:04.120
But you could try to detect
what a person is feeling.

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:07.560
And you can translate that in a way
and then give it to someone else.

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:13.840
So you can imagine this as a communication
pathway that wouldn't just work for words,

00:42:13.880 --> 00:42:16.440
but it would work for images,
it would work for feelings.

00:42:16.480 --> 00:42:20.960
It would work for anything that we share,
in a sense, where we can say because we're

00:42:20.960 --> 00:42:25.360
all humans, we all have feelings
of happiness and of sadness and of pain,

00:42:25.400 --> 00:42:28.400
and we have a smell and we have vision.

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:31.800
So you can transfer smells,
you can transfer vision,

00:42:31.840 --> 00:42:34.480
you can transfer auditory,
you can eventually,

00:42:34.520 --> 00:42:39.480
with enough translation,
even translate knowledge and memory.

00:42:39.520 --> 00:42:42.400
Connecting brains is going to be done,

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:43.960
there are definitely hurdles and it's

00:42:44.000 --> 00:42:47.400
going to take very important
technological breakthroughs.

00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:50.400
I think in the future we will definitely

00:42:50.440 --> 00:42:54.880
have very powerful synthetic
telepathy technologies.

00:43:02.840 --> 00:43:05.240
It will be possible not only to read

00:43:05.280 --> 00:43:10.280
others thoughts,
but also to manipulate them.

00:43:10.360 --> 00:43:12.320
Let's think about it a little bit more

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:21.000
about the impact and repercussions of this
new knowledge to society, to humanity.

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:26.440
We can, using mice, we cannot read activity
of every neuron here in the brain of a mouse,

00:43:26.440 --> 00:43:28.320
but if you look at the visual cortex,

00:43:28.360 --> 00:43:32.120
you can actually read the activity
of a few thousand neurons there.

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:38.080
And we can decipher some of the patterns
of activity as they relate to what

00:43:38.120 --> 00:43:41.320
the animal is seeing,
what the animal is looking at.

00:43:41.360 --> 00:43:46.360
And then we can go in with optical
methods, with optogenetics, and selectively

00:43:46.400 --> 00:43:51.720
change the patterns of activity and fool
the animal into thinking that he's saying

00:43:51.760 --> 00:43:56.040
one thing or the other thing
by essentially playing the piano with this

00:43:56.080 --> 00:43:59.560
neural circuits in the visual
cortex of the brain.

00:43:59.600 --> 00:44:01.680
This is something we can do today,

00:44:06.280 --> 00:44:08.840
people could do this

00:44:08.880 --> 00:44:11.320
with humans, not today.

00:44:11.360 --> 00:44:16.080
We don't have the methods to control,
to read the activity

00:44:16.120 --> 00:44:19.960
of many neurons in the brain
of humans and to change that.

00:44:20.000 --> 00:44:21.760
But these methods are going to be

00:44:21.800 --> 00:44:24.080
developed because we need
them for the patients.

00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:27.240
In fact, there's a project supported

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:31.760
by DARPA, by the foundation
of Military Research to build

00:44:31.800 --> 00:44:37.080
an electronic chip to record the activity
of 1 million neurons

00:44:37.120 --> 00:44:43.400
in a human, in a human patient and to
stimulate 1000 neurons at the same time.

00:44:43.440 --> 00:44:45.360
And this is not science fiction.

00:44:45.400 --> 00:44:48.600
This is something that's
being built as we speak.

00:44:48.640 --> 00:44:50.360
In fact, we're helping to build them

00:44:50.400 --> 00:44:53.480
because they need to use
this for patients.

00:44:53.520 --> 00:44:57.400
And this could be in the
clinic in four years.

00:45:04.640 --> 00:45:08.840
A whole new world of opportunities
is opening up before our eyes.

00:45:08.880 --> 00:45:14.560
A world where we will have the ability
to coevolve with artificial intelligence.

00:45:14.600 --> 00:45:16.480
There is even a hypothesis

00:45:16.520 --> 00:45:20.760
that in the distant future we won't
need our biological structures.

00:45:20.800 --> 00:45:22.920
If all we are are our thoughts,

00:45:22.960 --> 00:45:28.640
our memories, our emotions, our whole
self can be transferred into a computer.

00:45:28.680 --> 00:45:32.920
Our biological bodies will
no longer be necessary.

00:45:32.960 --> 00:45:35.480
So now we're getting into the more far

00:45:35.520 --> 00:45:39.000
future science fiction aspect
of this whole endeavor.

00:45:39.040 --> 00:45:42.280
There's a science fiction
term called mind uploading.

00:45:47.240 --> 00:45:52.000
What it means is that mind or who we are,
is they no longer dependent

00:45:52.040 --> 00:45:55.080
on the biological brain as such,
you still need a brain,

00:45:55.080 --> 00:45:59.560
there's no such thing
as no device running your mind.

00:45:59.600 --> 00:46:02.200
But it could be all sorts of devices.

00:46:02.240 --> 00:46:04.280
It could be a device that is better suited

00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:08.040
for, say, for example,
living in space or living on another

00:46:08.080 --> 00:46:11.280
planet or living underwater
or something like that.

00:46:15.720 --> 00:46:18.000
So when you get to a far future where

00:46:18.040 --> 00:46:21.760
the technology of whole brain emulation,
which is really the more scientific

00:46:21.800 --> 00:46:23.680
concept, what is it you're
actually doing here?

00:46:23.720 --> 00:46:27.720
You're trying to emulate what's going
on in the circuitry when you have that,

00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:32.240
then you can start thinking about
the personal and social

00:46:32.280 --> 00:46:36.560
consequences of doing that, which is
really what mind uploading means.

00:46:36.600 --> 00:46:38.680
It's a very philosophical term in a sense.

00:46:38.720 --> 00:46:43.280
Like what happens if you can take who I
am, my mind and move me to something else.

00:46:43.320 --> 00:46:46.560
So many things are then
potentially possible.

00:46:46.600 --> 00:46:48.600
You could make backups of your mind so

00:46:48.600 --> 00:46:54.000
that if something happens, you can
restore and it's not as devastating.

00:47:02.960 --> 00:47:05.080
The knowledge we have acquired in recent

00:47:05.120 --> 00:47:08.400
years about the brain has
given us possibilities

00:47:08.440 --> 00:47:09.440
until recently

00:47:09.480 --> 00:47:14.400
considered science fiction.
We're advancing at very high speed.

00:47:14.440 --> 00:47:16.240
We can't know where a deeper knowledge

00:47:16.280 --> 00:47:17.880
of the brain will lead us,

00:47:17.880 --> 00:47:22.360
but it's in our
hands to steer it in the right direction.

00:47:24.840 --> 00:47:27.880
It's critical to have

00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:30.920
guidelines, ethical guidelines,

00:47:30.920 --> 00:47:32.920
for the development
of these technologies.

00:47:33.600 --> 00:47:40.240
We propose to add to the human
Universal Declaration of Human Rights,

00:47:40.280 --> 00:47:45.240
new rights to protect
individuals from the abuse

00:47:45.280 --> 00:47:49.000
of neurotechnologies
and artificial intelligence.

00:47:49.040 --> 00:47:52.480
So this is what we call the neurorights,

00:47:52.520 --> 00:47:57.720
and they have to do with protecting
the mental Privacy of people.

00:47:57.760 --> 00:47:59.640
So this is our first one.

00:47:59.680 --> 00:48:04.200
Not just the data Privacy of our phone,
no, the mental Privacy, our thoughts,

00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:09.000
and even worse, our subconscious thoughts,
things that we don't even know,

00:48:09.040 --> 00:48:11.240
we're not aware of knowing them.

00:48:11.280 --> 00:48:13.640
Those should be protected,

00:48:13.680 --> 00:48:17.920
this is the ultimate human right,
our mental privacy.

00:48:21.880 --> 00:48:24.000
We should also have protection as we

00:48:24.040 --> 00:48:28.640
connect ourselves to computers
through brain computer interfaces.

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:30.320
Look at the effect that connecting us

00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:33.360
to the Net through our iPhones
has had in our culture.

00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:37.880
Well, imagine if that connection could be
direct through some brain computer

00:48:37.920 --> 00:48:44.720
interface that could be maybe
wireless and non-invasive,

00:48:44.760 --> 00:48:47.400
that could happen in the future.

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:51.680
So, this is going to affect our
sense of identity, of who we are,

00:48:51.720 --> 00:48:56.640
we need to protect the sense
of identity, the I, the self.

00:48:56.640 --> 00:49:00.520
This is something that humans never
had to worry about in the past,

00:49:00.520 --> 00:49:03.240
no one ever had that problem before.

00:49:07.680 --> 00:49:11.440
Related to this is also our
sense of agency or free will.

00:49:11.480 --> 00:49:15.480
It could be someone who wants to actually
change the way you're operating.

00:49:15.520 --> 00:49:20.360
So that's another fundamental
right that has to be protected.

00:49:25.600 --> 00:49:27.360
So, these are the neurorights.

00:49:27.400 --> 00:49:30.120
Now we have to convince

00:49:30.400 --> 00:49:32.240
the leaders of the world,

00:49:32.280 --> 00:49:35.680
the leaders of the society,
that this is a very important issue.

00:49:35.680 --> 00:49:40.720
We're discussing the future of our
society, the future of humankind,

00:49:40.760 --> 00:49:44.800
and that we have to make sure that these
technologies, which are so powerful

00:49:44.840 --> 00:49:51.320
and they could cause so much good,
not just in patients, but among ourselves,

00:49:51.360 --> 00:49:53.360
we'd be able to finally
understand who we are.

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:59.520
It's like a new Renaissance that these
technologies are not used against us.

00:49:59.560 --> 00:50:03.120
We have to be vigilant,
and we just have to put this ethical

00:50:03.160 --> 00:50:07.160
framework in place so
that this doesn't happen.

00:50:11.920 --> 00:50:17.000
This understanding is just going to give
us the tools to develop in many different

00:50:17.040 --> 00:50:22.160
directions, to explore in many directions,
and also to be versatile and adaptable so

00:50:22.200 --> 00:50:26.480
that maybe the human species has a really
long term future, not the kind of future

00:50:26.520 --> 00:50:31.120
where it works for a while, and then we're
not adapted well enough, and that's it.

00:50:31.160 --> 00:50:33.120
But a really long term future.

00:50:33.160 --> 00:50:35.640
That's what I'm hoping that this
could ultimately lead to.

00:50:35.680 --> 00:50:38.080
I hope it could provide us with the tools

00:50:38.120 --> 00:50:42.400
to have basically what you might
call another Cambrian explosion,

00:50:42.440 --> 00:50:47.000
where the new species develop in all
kinds of directions, except it's us.

00:50:47.040 --> 00:50:49.800
It's the human species deciding that we

00:50:49.840 --> 00:50:53.760
want to try out this direction,
try out that direction, and so forth.

00:50:58.560 --> 00:51:00.440
Science can change the world.

00:51:00.480 --> 00:51:01.520
It can change society.

00:51:01.560 --> 00:51:03.000
It can change the course of history,

00:51:03.040 --> 00:51:04.920
and I can do that for good or for bad.

00:51:04.960 --> 00:51:09.240
It's up to us to make sure that it
goes in the right direction.

00:51:14.320 --> 00:51:18.640
We've entered a new age,
the age of neuroscience.

00:51:18.680 --> 00:51:24.920
An age in which the way we experience
and feel the world will radically change.

00:51:24.960 --> 00:51:27.800
Welcome to the world of tomorrow.

